---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import Stamp from "../components/Stamp.astro";

const facts = [
  { term: "Build", detail: "Astro, static output. No server, no database." },
  { term: "Styling", detail: "Tailwind CSS v4, with the token set shared with lantip.space." },
  { term: "Type", detail: "EB Garamond Variable and Fragment Mono, self-hosted through Fontsource." },
  { term: "Ornament", detail: "Procedural guilloche, generated in the lantip.space repo." },
  { term: "Tracking", detail: "None. No analytics, no cookies, no newsletter." },
  { term: "CV", detail: "One YAML file, validated at build, rendered to two PDFs." },
  { term: "JavaScript", detail: "None shipped. The site is HTML and CSS." },
];
---
<Base
  title="Colophon — Azzadiva Sawungrana"
  description="How this site is built: Astro, Tailwind, self-hosted type, procedural ornament, no tracking."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6">
    <header class="mt-10 md:mt-14">
      <p class="stamp-text text-ink-soft">colophon</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-[2rem] leading-[1.12] text-ink-deep md:text-[2.5rem]">
        A quiet site, because it does not need to be loud.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-[1.08rem]">
        This is the sibling of{" "}
        <a href="https://lantip.space">lantip.space</a>, which holds the tools. This one holds
        the record. They share a design language because maintaining two idioms for one person
        is a waste of a good afternoon.
      </p>
    </header>

    <Rule />

    <dl class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {
        facts.map((f) => (
          <div class="border-t rule-line pt-2">
            <dt class="stamp-text text-ink-soft">{f.term}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-1 text-[0.98rem] text-ink">{f.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <Rule />

    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-4 pb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
        <Stamp label="on the CV" />
      </div>
      <p>
        The CV is not written twice. Everything on <a href="/cv">/cv</a>, and both PDFs, comes
        from a single YAML file that a schema validates at build time. If a field is missing or a
        date is malformed, the build fails rather than shipping something wrong to a stranger.
      </p>
      <p>
        There are two PDFs on purpose. One is designed, on paper, for a person. The other is
        plain, white and single-column, for the software that many large employers use to read
        applications first. A beautiful CV that a parser cannot read is a CV nobody sees.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[0.92rem] text-ink-soft">
        Design and content are mine. The paper texture, the ink and terracotta palette, and the
        ornament generator come from lantip.space. Nothing on this site is tracked, and there is
        no cookie to consent to.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
